<section id="start-with-care-level">
<h2>1. Start with care level, not amenities</h2>

<p>The most common mistake families make when starting a senior living search is to lead with amenities — the dining room, the activity calendar, the courtyard. These are the things communities market most aggressively because they are the easiest to photograph and the least likely to prompt difficult follow-up questions.</p>

<p>Start instead with care level. Identify what your family member needs right now: help with bathing and dressing (assisted living), structured dementia care in a secured environment (memory care), 24-hour skilled nursing for complex medical needs (skilled nursing facility), or primarily companionship and a maintenance-free lifestyle (independent living). Then think about the likely next stage of need — and whether the community you are considering can support it without requiring a move.</p>

<p>A move during a health crisis is one of the most disorienting experiences for an older adult with cognitive decline. Choosing a community that can accommodate increasing needs — or that is part of a campus with multiple care levels — is not just a practical decision. It is a kindness.</p>

<p>Once care level is confirmed, amenities become relevant. A beautiful outdoor terrace matters a great deal for an independent living resident. It matters significantly less if the person you are placing has advanced dementia and will rarely access it.</p>
</section>

<section id="licensing-and-oversight">
<h2>2. Licensing and oversight first</h2>

<p>Before you tour, look up the license. Every assisted living community, memory care unit, and nursing home in the United States is licensed by a state agency. That agency conducts inspections, investigates complaints, and — depending on the state — publishes those records publicly. The license number is the starting point for all of this research.</p>

<p>SilverTech surfaces state license numbers and CMS certification numbers on each facility listing. The state regulatory hub pages link directly to each state's verification tool and inspection database. Pull the most recent inspection report before your tour. Look for the nature of any deficiencies — not just whether deficiencies exist, but what they were about. A deficiency related to documentation is different from one related to medication errors or resident safety.</p>

<p>For nursing homes, the CMS Five-Star Quality Rating system is the most accessible starting point. The rating covers health inspections, staffing levels, and quality measures. A five-star rating does not guarantee quality; a one-star rating is a serious warning. But read the sub-ratings separately — a community can have a high overall rating while scoring poorly on health inspections specifically, which are based on actual surveyor findings rather than self-reported data.</p>

<p>Regulatory standing is not a perfect proxy for quality. Good communities occasionally receive deficiencies; struggling communities sometimes pass inspections. But licensing history is objective data. Brand claims about "person-centered care" and "family-first philosophy" are not.</p>
</section>

<section id="staffing">
<h2>3. Staffing questions that matter</h2>

<p>Staffing is the single strongest predictor of resident experience and outcomes that a family can assess during a tour. The physical environment matters. The programming matters. But if staffing is inadequate — in numbers, in training, or in stability — none of the rest of it holds up under pressure.</p>

<p>Ask these questions directly and watch how they are answered:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>What is the staff-to-resident ratio on the day shift, evening shift, and overnight?</strong> Ratios vary by care type and state regulation, but the evening and overnight numbers are where you will find the most variation — and often the most concern.</li>
  <li><strong>How long has the director of nursing been in this role?</strong> High turnover in clinical leadership is one of the clearest signals of operational instability. If the current DON has been there less than a year, ask who held the role before.</li>
  <li><strong>How do you cover a caregiver call-out?</strong> Communities that rely heavily on agency staff to cover call-outs have less consistent care delivery and often higher costs for families over time.</li>
  <li><strong>For memory care: what specific training do caregivers receive?</strong> Dementia care is a specialized skill. Ask about specific training programs, not general language about "person-centered care."</li>
  <li><strong>Are nurses on-site 24/7 or on-call?</strong> In many assisted living communities, nurses are not present overnight. Ask who responds to a medical event at 2 a.m.</li>
</ul>

<p>If a tour guide becomes evasive or redirects to the amenities when you ask staffing questions, that is itself a data point.</p>
</section>

<section id="daily-life">
<h2>4. Daily life and community fit</h2>

<p>Once care level is confirmed and regulatory standing is acceptable, daily life quality becomes the deciding factor. The best way to assess this is not to review the activity calendar — it is to visit during a normal weekday and observe.</p>

<p>What are residents doing at 10 a.m. on a Tuesday? Are they engaged and mobile, or are they parked in front of a television? Is there visible interaction between staff and residents, or do staff seem task-focused and residents seem isolated? These observations are more reliable than any tour script.</p>

<p>Talk to current residents if you can. Ask them what they like and what they would change. Ask a family member of a current resident — not one the community has provided as a reference, but one you find in the parking lot or common areas. Families who are genuinely satisfied will tell you. Families who are not satisfied will tell you something different.</p>

<p>For memory care specifically: observe the environment during a transition time — a meal, a shift change, or an activity ending. These are the moments when behavioral symptoms are most likely to surface. A well-run memory care unit manages these transitions with calm, consistent structure. A poorly-run unit shows friction and dysregulation at transition points.</p>
</section>

<section id="cost-transparency">
<h2>5. Cost transparency and contract terms</h2>

<p>Ask for the full written fee schedule before your tour ends. Not the base rate — the complete fee schedule including every service that can be billed separately. This document, more than anything else the community shows you, reveals how the business is actually structured.</p>

<p>Common add-on charges that significantly increase actual monthly costs include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Care level tier fees (often $500–$2,000 per month above the base rate)</li>
  <li>Medication management fees (flat monthly or per-medication)</li>
  <li>Incontinence supply fees</li>
  <li>Transportation fees</li>
  <li>Laundry fees</li>
  <li>One-time community fees at move-in (often $1,500–$5,000, typically non-refundable)</li>
</ul>

<p>Ask how often rates have increased and by how much over the past three years. Ask what the process is if care needs increase beyond what the community can manage — and what that transition looks like logistically and financially. Ask about the move-out notice policy and what happens if a resident needs to leave quickly due to a medical event.</p>

<p>Read the residency agreement before signing. Have it reviewed by an elder law attorney if there is any financial complexity, a CCRC entrance fee involved, or if you have concerns about specific terms. A residency agreement is a contract. Treat it like one.</p>
</section>

<section id="compare-three">
<h2>6. Compare three options before deciding</h2>

<p>Tour at least three communities before making a decision. This is not about being indecisive — it is about having enough contrast to see real differences rather than perceiving one community through the lens of the tour guide who showed it to you.</p>

<p>After each tour, record your observations in writing: staffing answers, inspection summary, fee schedule review, and your own impressions of the resident environment. Do this the same day — details fade quickly and communities blur together when you are under emotional and time pressure.</p>

<p>When comparing across communities, weight the factors in this order: care level match, regulatory standing, staffing model, cost structure, then daily life and amenities. The communities that look best on the first three criteria rarely look worst on the last two. The inverse is also usually true.</p>
</section>

<section id="red-flags">
<h2>7. Red flags to watch for</h2>

<p>These are not disqualifying on their own, but they warrant follow-up before you proceed:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Evasiveness on staffing ratios or turnover rates</li>
  <li>Refusal to provide the full written fee schedule before a deposit</li>
  <li>Pressure to sign or hold a unit before you have completed your research</li>
  <li>Recent ownership or management change (within the last 12 months)</li>
  <li>Multiple deficiencies in the most recent inspection report related to resident safety, medication management, or abuse/neglect</li>
  <li>A director of nursing who has been in the role less than six months</li>
  <li>High use of agency staff for regular coverage (not just emergencies)</li>
  <li>Residents who appear sedated, isolated, or visibly unstimulated during a weekday visit</li>
</ul>

<p>A single red flag warrants a conversation. Multiple red flags warrant finding a different community.</p>
</section>

<section id="checklist">
<h2>Decision checklist</h2>

<p>Before signing a residency agreement, confirm:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Care level is confirmed as appropriate for current and near-future needs</li>
  <li>License is current and in good standing with the state agency</li>
  <li>Most recent inspection report has been reviewed</li>
  <li>Staffing ratios on all shifts have been provided in writing</li>
  <li>Full fee schedule has been reviewed, not just the base rate</li>
  <li>Rate increase history for the past three years has been provided</li>
  <li>Care transition policy has been confirmed in writing</li>
  <li>Residency agreement has been reviewed by an attorney if appropriate</li>
  <li>A normal weekday visit (not a tour event) has been completed</li>
  <li>At least two other communities have been toured for comparison</li>
</ul>
</section>

